marikar said he joins me here on the  show mr. Mehra thanks for joining me I  appreciate it um let me ask you if  cursory right here why did you want the  Olympics in LA you know I think Olympics  are in our blood  I'm an 84 boy who grew up as a kid when  the world came to my town watch Carl  Lewis and Jones annoyed and Oscar De La  Hoya and everybody who in 1984 kind of  showed off the best of the city and made  a profit that we put into youth sports  that discovered people like the Williams  sisters so you know I think that unlike  a lot of places we've got infrastructure  built here it's a private bid no public  money we make money and we're great  sports down with all the facilities  pretty much done so it seemed like a  no-brainer for us to go to the Olympics  and say just like we did in 84 it's time  to reboot the brand come to LA we'll  reconnect you with a new generation of  sports fans and of sports and you know  after four years we got it and you know  for the first time coming back since  1996 at the Summer Games United States  Boston of course punted on the Olympics  they didn't want it after they were  named as the US Representative for the  2024 Olympics have you noted over the  last you know four eight years whatever  timetable that interest in hosting the  Olympics has kind of waned globally  absolutely I mean founded Boston Park  Hamburg Germany dropped out Rome Italy  Budapest Hungary so it was down to just  Paris and us and this is a great way  that we both can win Paris will take 24  will take 28 but we'll get hundreds of  millions of dollars more for waiting and  you know that's what my pitch to the  Olympics was this is kind of yearly like  the mid seventies remember 1977 we did  it was right after the Montreal Olympics  were you know that said he went way into  debt to build new facilities that  weren't used afterwards as extensively  as they thought and so actually no other  city in the world was around at the end  that we got to 84 Olympics but after la  showed how to do it with Peter Ueberroth  and changing the sponsorship model in  1985 six cities bid in Barcelona won for  their 92 Olympics so I think the same  thing will happen after la people will  see oh you know you don't have to kill  yourself you can use existing  infrastructure don't try to build a  bunch of vampy for using you see  say for instance for our athletes  village which saved us probably a couple  billion dollars we're using the RAM  Stadium instead of you know building a  new Olympic Stadium which saves us  probably two or three billion dollars so  these are there's a smarter way we hope  to because I love the Olympics this town  loves the Olympics but eighty to ninety  percent of people support this in  different polls so it's time to help the  Olympics come back and get back to its  roots which is about sports and  competition when Boston was awarded the  Olympics locals the media they pretty  much universally panned it and and  didn't want it what has been the  reaction of your constituents the people  of LA to your bid and ultimately your  awarding of the Olympics it is it's such  overwhelming support I mean I think with  our lowest poll was 78 percent the  highest has been 88% one that just came  out last week by the local university  showed 83 percent support I mean in a  big city that means you can sign  hundreds of thousands of people against  it but there's millions of people for it  because you know so many people  volunteered so many people went to those  events even some people who don't care  for sports remember there was no traffic  in LA for 17 days and 84 so some of them  come up to me saying hey mayor we throw  the Olympics just so I can get 17 days  with no traffic back here in LA so it's  an amazing things that I think brings us  together and the real legacy of this  will not be a bunch of buildings it will  be we're going to make sports  universally accessible to all kids in  all zip codes so no family has to stress  out about $150 for a uniform that they  can't afford so their kid doesn't get to  play baseball or basketball and that  legacy won't have to wait till after the  Olympics our negotiations for 28 games  enough money to start that as year early  as next year in the lead up and then  permanently after how Bad's trap are  going to be in the build-up to the  Olympics though you know traffic is  always bad in Los Angeles but voters in  November passed the biggest initiative  in this nation's history times to we're  building 15 new light rail lines and  rapid transit lines fixing our freeways  I mean LA is a place people wanna be so  traffic's always going to be a little  bit tough but you know I joke that it's  not just the Olympics it's a Jewish  holiday or Farmageddon enough people get  off the road here about 10 percent and  the traffic actually flows so we hope to  see a lot  stuff open by 2028 the new lines  certainly where the villages at UCLA  will have a subway that can move faster  than a car even when there's no traffic  on the west side of LA all the way to  downtown where a lot of those events are  going to be at USC and and down by the  Staples Center well the Olympics be  spread out over areas outside of Los  Angeles how far outside of the greater  LA area do you expect to go there's a  couple of things outside of LA but  supposed to clustered for Olympic parks  which will allow you to see multiple  sports without having to go in and out  of security you can see probably no  fencing right next to basketball and  still stay in the same secured area  downtown down by the StubHub Center and  Carson can check out multiple sports  some tennis to rugby and other things  that will be in the same campus the only  ones outside the LA area is right now we  have volleyball wanted to be down in  where the Ducks played the Honda Center  in Anaheim because that's kind of the  center of basketball globally i sorry  volleyball globally and then our rowing  you don't have a lot of water so right  now we're a little bit east of LA and  the lake a place called Lake Paris but  we're also talking about possibly doing  in Long Beach we're going to have four  or five different sports already so  it'll be pretty compact once you get  into an Olympic Park and the nice thing  that's different than other Olympics you  know even when they're closer together  you got to go in and out of security not  going to take hours once you get in here  you might catch three or four sports and  any one of these parks before let you go  as we talked about the Olympics the  interest in hosting  Summer Games Winter Games has waned kind  of globally do you think the Olympics  should continue to rotate cities every  four years or would it benefit from  having a fixed location for summer and  winter games you know I'm open to both  and LA would be a natural place to have  you know every four Olympics come here  we're ready we could I said you know 30  minutes probably two months from now or  two decades from now but that said I  hope that LA inspires cities that might  think I can't do this that you can fit  your city to the Olympics  rather than having Olympics having to  fit into your city so to me you know  there's plenty of places that have  plenty of facilities if we would just  get over the oh I have to have 15  people watch team handball or whatever  it is you know you can have two or three  thousand in a great arena that probably  exists in American cities and anyone  have to build things same thing around  the world so I think the Olympics can be  a little bit more accommodating realize  that the real audience is the TV  audience and you can have a great  smaller venue we could still keep going  to new cities but if they decide to just  go to some set ones la would be at the  top of that list well congratulations  mr. mayor on the Olympics 2028 best of  luck in preparing for it and thanks for  joining me here on the show thanks man  really appreciate it good talking to  Matt Eric Garcetti the mayor of Los  Angeles joining me here dan Patrick Sean  the Dan Patrick show weekday mornings on  audience

